{
  "readme": "Shared corpus proving the product invariant of PD-043: a saved TicketFilter selects the same set in apps/web (matchesFilter, over rows the API already translated) and in apps/api (ticketFilterConditions, over columns holding the EI's own words). Both suites read this file and assert the same expected ids, so a divergence fails a test instead of surfacing as a node that announces one number and lists another. `stored` is what the column holds, `client` is what the row carries after the API translates; a case's filter is always written in the client's words, which is what a saved view contains. A case may also carry a window (awake | sleeping | all), applied with today: the two tickets due on 09-02 and 09-03 sit on either side of the SLEEP_DAYS cut, so a side that moves the constant alone fails here. A day is a São Paulo calendar day, the operation's, whatever instant the column holds: the ticket due at 01:30Z on 09-03 is still 09-02 in São Paulo, so it is awake. A ticket's subject is not a field of this file: each side builds it from `title`, `carrierName`, `client.product` and `beneficiaryName`, the web through `buildSubject` and the API through the `SUBJECT` expression, so a `subjectQuery` case proves the two constructions agree and not merely that both read the same column. A ticket with no `title` takes the derived subject; one with all four missing takes its own id.",
